# Everything here is read by FreeBSD: shell scripts, rc scripts, Python and
# configuration files. A CRLF checkout on Windows must not turn into a CRLF file
# on the firewall, where /bin/sh would read the carriage return as part of the
# command and an rc script would fail in a way nobody would guess from the error.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary

# What an appliance carries is the archive of a commit - the tarball its update downloads, and what
# xgs-image-builder puts into an image (its fetch-project.sh takes `git archive` of the commit) - and
# it is the driver's and the plugin's sources, install/, kernel-sources/, the changelog the About
# page reads, the licence, and the tools that are run on a firewall: the coprocessor's counter
# readers, the sensors, the UDP sender. Nothing else is read there, so nothing else goes:
#
# - the site (npuforge.org) and the problem reports' relay, services of their own deployed from here
#   by their own workflows (2026-10-22)
# - the documentation and its photographs, nine tenths of the tree (2026-10-22)
# - the workflows, the files about the project itself, and compat.json, which only a workflow reads
# - the repository's own tools - the checks, the generators, the tests, the disassembly helpers -
#   which run here and in CI, from a checkout; and the capture script, which runs on the vendor's
#   firmware, not on OPNsense
#
# Until 2026-10-22 an image took the whole clone, these and the site included, whatever this said.
website/ export-ignore
relay/ export-ignore
docs/ export-ignore
.github/ export-ignore
compat.json export-ignore
CITATION.cff export-ignore
CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md export-ignore
CONTRIBUTING.md export-ignore
DESIGN.md export-ignore
RELEASING.md export-ignore
SECURITY.md export-ignore
tools/*.py export-ignore
tools/sfos-capture.sh export-ignore
